POSNETEK_A = REC(
    "A beach lifeguard",
    "You will listen to an interview with Ben Pascoe, who works as a lifeguard on a beach in Cornwall.",
    """Interviewer: Ben, you've been a lifeguard in Cornwall for nine summers. What does a typical day look like?

Ben: We start at nine, when we put up the red and yellow flags. People should only swim between those flags, because that's the area we watch most closely. Then we check the equipment – the boards, the rescue tubes, the first-aid kit – and we look at the sea. The conditions can change completely from one hour to the next, so we're watching the water all day, until we finish at six.

Interviewer: How did you become a lifeguard?

Ben: I grew up in a village near the beach and spent every summer in the water. When I was sixteen, a friend of mine got caught in a rip current – that's a strong current that pulls you away from the shore. A lifeguard swam out and brought him back. I decided there and then that I wanted to do the same.

Interviewer: What kind of training do you need?

Ben: You have to be a very strong swimmer – you need to be able to swim four hundred metres in under seven and a half minutes. Then there's a course that covers first aid, rescue techniques and how to read the sea. It's quite tough, and every year we have to pass the fitness test again.

Interviewer: What are the most common problems on the beach?

Ben: Honestly, most of our work is not dramatic rescues. We treat a lot of jellyfish stings and cuts from rocks, and we find lost children – on a busy day in August, that can happen ten times. But rip currents are the biggest danger. Every summer we rescue people who were swimming outside the flags and got pulled out to sea.

Interviewer: What should people do if they're caught in a rip current?

Ben: The most important thing is not to panic and not to try to swim straight back to the beach against the current, because you'll just get exhausted. Instead, swim parallel to the shore until you're out of the current, or if you can't, raise your hand and shout for help. Rip currents don't pull you under the water – they just pull you away from the beach.

Interviewer: Do you remember your most difficult rescue?

Ben: Yes. Three years ago, a father and his two children were caught in a rip on a very windy day. The waves were about two metres high. My colleague and I went out on boards and managed to bring all three back. The younger child was only seven. Afterwards, the family sent us a letter every Christmas.

Interviewer: What's the best part of the job?

Ben: Being outdoors all day, and the team. We're like a family. And of course the feeling when you bring someone back safely – nothing compares to that.

Interviewer: Any advice for people going to the beach this summer?

Ben: Always swim at a beach with lifeguards, swim between the flags, and if you're not sure about the conditions, ask us. That's what we're there for.""")

POSNETEK_B = REC(
    "The science of happiness",
    "You will listen to a conversation with psychologist Dr Helen Price and student Jamal Hussein, who took part in a university course on happiness.",
    """Presenter: More and more universities are now offering courses on happiness. Helen, you teach one of them. What do students actually learn?

Helen: The course is based on scientific research into what makes people happy – and what doesn't. Most people believe that they'd be happier if they had more money, a better job or a perfect body. But the research shows that these things make much less difference than we expect. Once our basic needs are met, extra money only makes us a little happier, and only for a short time.

Presenter: So what does make us happy?

Helen: The strongest factor in almost every study is relationships. People who have close friends and family, and who spend time with them, are much happier than people who don't. Other important factors are helping others, getting enough sleep, exercising and spending time in nature.

Presenter: Jamal, why did you take the course?

Jamal: To be honest, because I was quite unhappy in my first year at university. I'd moved to a new city, I didn't know anyone, and I spent most evenings alone in my room on my phone. A friend told me about the course, and I thought, why not?

Presenter: What did you have to do?

Jamal: Every week we had a task. One week we had to write down three good things that had happened each day. Another week we had to do something kind for a stranger – I paid for someone's coffee in a café, and she was so surprised that she nearly cried. We also had to sleep at least seven hours a night and keep a record.

Helen: The tasks are important, because knowing what makes you happy isn't enough. You have to change your habits, and that takes practice – a bit like going to the gym.

Presenter: Did it work, Jamal?

Jamal: It really did. The biggest change came from joining a club. We were told to start one new social activity, so I joined the university's hiking club. Now I go walking every Sunday with people who've become my closest friends. I also use my phone much less. I deleted two social media apps that were making me compare myself with others.

Presenter: Helen, is there anything that surprised you in the research?

Helen: Gratitude. Simply writing a short letter to thank someone who has helped you can make you happier for weeks. When students read their letters aloud to the person, the effect is even stronger.

Presenter: What's one thing listeners could do today?

Helen: Put your phone away during dinner and talk to the people you're with.

Jamal: And go outside. Even a ten-minute walk in the park makes a difference.""")

